{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on exact head 5cc8c17ba84618165f45ecaa1851bb056a32108b, primary packages/ui/README.md, resting on all 52 supplied artifacts.\n\nThe four screens, scenario controls, light/dark layouts, phone layouts, visible keyboard focus and one-adapter boundary form a useful complete UI foundation. I independently ran root typecheck, all 33 candidate component tests and all 6 browser smoke tests; all passed. I inspected the ten screenshots. Six additional checker reproductions in the detached worktree pass by asserting the defects below; they are diagnostic checks, not acceptance tests.\n\n1. P1 — The policy preview and the exported policy give different answers (src/room/dryrun.ts, compileDraft and dryRun). With an accepted agent claim scoped to [\"**\"], a refuse-claim draft for [\"migrations/**\"] predicts a refusal, but evaluating its emitted JSONata with the pinned policy evaluator returns false. The preview uses glob overlap while the expression searches for a stripped prefix inside the scope string. The screen says this is a replay and shows the expression as the policy-file rule, so a user can adopt a rule believing broad claims are blocked when they are not. Make prediction and the actual compiled rule use identical, profile-compatible semantics; safely quote literal paths. If a draft cannot be compiled faithfully, explain that limitation instead of reporting a successful replay. Keep the four draft functions. Add comparisons with the real evaluator for broad, intersecting, disjoint and quoted patterns; do not silently add an unprofiled JSONata function.\n\n2. P1 — Publication recovery is described incorrectly for compromised keys and unexpected writers (screens/Room.tsx OpCard, room/live/describe.ts, and the blanket statements about acts after reservation). Every unresolved op is described as \"main still reads as before\" and \"keeps pushing ... forward\", even when readBack.main is \"unexpected\" and an AbortAttempt records a compromised-key trigger and token revocation. An injected valid unresolved variant reproduces that false instruction. Render the discriminated read-back and abort facts. Expected-main without compromise can describe continued publication of the reserved commit; unexpected main requires held-slot reconciliation, and compromised evidence stops forward retries while abort/read-back/revert recovery proceeds. Show known token-revocation and revert-lane outcomes. Qualify the ordinary after-reservation rule with its compromise exception. Cover these branches in component tests.\n\n3. P2 — Failed landing cards omit the rule, reason and fix (screens/Room.tsx OpCard). A failed op with reason.code=\"refused\" shows only Failed: neither its rule nor its fix appears. Retryable, conflict/check-failed and aborted terminal variants likewise get no state detail from this switch. Show the existing RefusalNotice for refused failures, the recorded fix for retryable outcomes, and relevant conflict, failed-check and abort facts. This is part of the requested \"every refusal names the rule and fix\" behavior, rather than a request for new transport methods.\n\n4. P2 — Historical note anchors acquire an unsupported claim about the current head (screens/Proposal.tsx FileView, lines 268–272 and the \"this line has not changed since\" label). changedSince=null is treated as unchanged, so generation-1 notes are projected onto generation 2 at the same line with that assurance despite unavailable interdiff data. The independent null-interdiff test reproduces this. The adapter only supplies the previous-generation interdiff, which also cannot establish that an older origin head is unchanged across several generations. Keep each thread bound to its recorded head/path/line. Project onto a newer head only with verified origin-to-current continuity or a correct line mapping; otherwise retain an accessible historical-head thread and say the relation is unknown. Test unavailable interdiff and an earlier changed generation followed by an unchanged one.\n\n5. P2 — A late explanation replaces the currently selected act's explanation (ui/WhyDialog.tsx useEffect). Request A, close it and request B; resolve B, then A. The dialog now displays A while B is the selected act. The deferred-response checker test reproduces this. Bind responses to the active request and invalidate old work on close/change/unmount. Handle a rejected explain call with an honest recoverable error rather than an unhandled rejection and permanent Loading. Verify reordered and failed responses.\n\n6. P2 — Incomplete live reads are presented as complete room facts (room/live/live-room.ts load, ui/format.ts unpublished and screens/Room.tsx status). The adapter ignores more/cursor on lane, attention and log pages. A first log page with head=1000 and publishedThrough=500 produces \"All published\" because lag is counted from loaded feed items; the same snapshot invents a free slot with last publication 0 when no held op appears in the loaded lanes. The fake-HttpRoom checker test reproduces both while all three pages report more=true. Follow the existing cursors or explicitly present partial data with continuation; derive publication lag from the authoritative log counters. Represent the unreadable publication slot as unavailable, not free, and avoid complete/empty queue claims from partial reads. This does not require a production transport or a new slot endpoint: the agreed live stub can report unavailable data honestly.\n\nArchitecture conclusion: keep the shared contract bridge, one RoomAdapter and deterministic scenario. The live HttpRoom/WebSocket stub is appropriate for this request; the documented missing reads are not grounds to demand the later full transport. Fix completeness and request/head provenance at the adapter/view boundary rather than adding shadows of Room authority.\n\nSecurity conclusion: the candidate does not request or display workspace tokens and uses escaped text rendering. Approval is withheld because a policy preview can overstate protection and compromise recovery currently promises forward retries contrary to the protocol. The frontend must report the Room's authority and outcomes accurately.\n\nSimplification conclusion: preserve the calm four-screen structure and all requested functionality. Centralize terminal-state explanations, unknown/partial data presentation and asynchronous request ownership; use the real policy semantics instead of two independent answers. Estimates do not narrow this repair scope. Refresh the contract bridge/README baseline against the merged contract amendment when preparing the repair head.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:9c67d44df99010e3b4d8bc77c7857725f4476d31","binding":"assigned","head":"5cc8c17ba84618165f45ecaa1851bb056a32108b","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3ec5dbe357c6881ff4e6550d41d6816fafd16e65\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:20cf77e0d8e8478ea9044953ed5903b698055dc1\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:242988aa57fe346d38f90f5e629a6b96b5cd7c6e","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}